{"kind":"artifact","text":"Cache compatibility coverage at internal/app@4981c6d0f571c9a7504311f83c6cb08517be0acd, fulfilling planner's request 160c9acb under my promise e9f89f9e. Companion to internal/workroom artifact 0c0c5bce.\n\nWHAT IS HERE. One test file, app_test.go. No product code in internal/app changed.\n\nWHY IT NEEDED REBUILDING RATHER THAN RE-ANCHORING. Planner's condition asked for the fold and profile version bumped with cache compatibility coverage rebuilt. The existing TestAnOlderProfileCacheIsRebuiltUnderTheNewRules could only seed a *missing field* — and said so in its own comment, that it \"cannot prove the case the profile exists for: rules that actually changed\". A cache test that can only detect a shape difference does not test what a profile version is for. Bumping the number and re-pointing that test at @12 would have produced a green gate proving nothing, which is the exact failure mode that has cost me two heads today.\n\nSo it is rebuilt. It now constructs a real merge through the app API — implementer, reviewer, ratified approval, prospective receipt, published successor, review request withdrawn before the merge — and seeds an @11-shaped cache holding the successor stale at birth. That is a genuine disagreement about the world between the two profiles, not a difference in field presence.\n\nBOTH DIRECTIONS PROVEN, because a cache test that passes for the wrong reason is worse than none. Reverting ProfileVersion to @11 fails with \"the rebuilt projection still has the merge successor stale at birth: the workroom-fold@11 cache was served instead of replayed\" — so the bump is genuinely what forces the replay. Removing the checkpoint rule fails with \"the successor is stale under the profile this build holds, so the seeded @11 answer below would be indistinguishable\" — so the fixture's cross-profile disagreement is real rather than assumed. The second mutation is the one that matters: it proves the test would notice if the two profiles agreed, which is the only way to know the fixture discriminates.\n\nTHE PRINCIPLE THIS ENCODES. A version bump that leaves a stale cache answering the old way is not a compatibility detail, it is the defect the version exists to prevent. I have shipped a stale committed UI embed three times on one branch, and it is the same shape: an artefact derived from source, committed alongside it, and silently permitted to disagree. Treating the cache as part of the contract rather than an implementation detail is the whole point of the condition.\n\nGATES at this exact head: gofmt, build and vet clean; go test -count=1 ./... across all 30 packages ok, including ./internal/app at 104s; git diff --check clean; worktree clean.\n\nONE THING I DID NOT ESTABLISH, marked unknown rather than assumed. There is a known deploy-order hazard for a previous schema bump — residents and adapters must be restarted on the new binary before rebuilding the CLI, or the old fold refuses new records. Whether @11 to @12 carries the same ordering constraint was not tested. The commit message and merge.md both say to restart residents and adapters at the merged commit, but that is guidance written from the earlier case rather than a verified property of this one. Nothing was restarted during this work, and the resident on port 7777 is still running the old binary. A reviewer should treat the deploy order as unverified.","body":{"commit":"4981c6d0f571c9a7504311f83c6cb08517be0acd","path":"internal/app"}}